\section{Which function do I need?}{
A data frame with a column of weights is \code{\link[=table_continuous]{table_continuous()}}
(\verb{weights = }). A survey design object -- strata, clusters, finite
population correction, calibration, replicate weights -- is this
function. Passing one to the other errors with the name of the
right one; there is no silent coercion, because the design-based
standard errors, degrees of freedom and tests cannot be recovered
from the weights alone.
}

\section{Two conventions, one bridge}{
\code{table_continuous(weights = )} implements the \strong{frequency-expansion}
convention: a weight is a number of copies, and \code{SD} has denominator
\code{sum(w) - 1}. This function implements the \strong{sampling-weight}
convention: a weight is a number of units represented, and \code{SD} is
\code{sqrt(survey::svyvar())}, whose denominator is \code{n - 1} on weights
normalised to sum to \code{n}. These are two estimands, not two
approximations of one.

\code{rescale = TRUE} is the bridge, and it is an identity rather than a
coincidence. Writing \verb{w' = w * n / sum(w)}, the rescaled weighted
variance is
\verb{sum(w' (x - xbar)^2) / (sum(w') - 1) = n / (n - 1) * sum(w (x - xbar)^2) / sum(w)},
which is what \code{survey::svyvar()} computes. So on a design that
declares nothing but weights, \code{table_continuous(weights = w, rescale = TRUE)} and this function return the same mean and the same
standard deviation. The default \code{rescale = FALSE} does not, and that
is the estimand boundary, not a bug.

The mean is continuous across both regimes: \verb{sum(w x) / sum(w)} does
not move when the weights are rescaled.
}

\section{Choosing the statistics}{
\code{show_columns} takes the tokens of \code{\link[=table_continuous]{table_continuous()}} with two
additions and one removal:
\itemize{
\item \code{"se"} -- the design-based standard error of the mean;
\item \code{"deff"} -- the design effect (requires \code{deff = TRUE});
\item \code{"med_ci"} is refused. The exact interval of the sibling inverts a
binomial sign test on independent observations, which a clustered
or stratified sample is not.
